WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
In the alcohol reactions video, ethanol was oxidised

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:07.040
under reflux, all the way to ethanoic acid.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:08.640
That's the acid in vinegar.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:11.040
Now compare it with propan-one-ol.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.440
Both have a molar mass of sixty, and

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:15.680
both contain an O H group.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.840
Yet the acid boils at one hundred and

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.440
eighteen degrees, and the alcohol at ninety seven.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.840
The acid turns blue litmus red, and it

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.680
fizzes with baking soda.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.320
The alcohol does neither.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.000
So what does the acid's group change?

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.080
The acid's group is the carboxyl group, C

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:35.440
O O H.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:38.320
It's a carbonyl, C double bond O, and

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.960
an O H, both on the same carbon.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.480
So the two oxygens aren't alike.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.920
One is double bonded to carbon, and only

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.680
the other carries a hydrogen.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:49.480
There's just one O H.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:52.800
Inside the molecule, these are covalent bonds, and

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:54.800
both the C double bond O and the

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:56.320
O H are polar.

00:00:57.534 --> 00:01:00.173
To name one, find the longest chain that

00:01:00.254 --> 00:01:02.493
includes the carboxyl carbon.

00:01:02.614 --> 00:01:05.213
Drop the alkane's final e, and add oic

00:01:05.374 --> 00:01:06.333
acid.

00:01:06.374 --> 00:01:09.613
The carboxyl carbon is always carbon one, so

00:01:09.654 --> 00:01:15.053
there's no number: methanoic, ethanoic, propanoic, butanoic acid.

00:01:15.214 --> 00:01:17.133
Count that carbon in the chain.

00:01:17.214 --> 00:01:19.613
Ethanoic acid has two carbons.

00:01:19.694 --> 00:01:22.093
Branches are numbered from the carboxyl end, so

00:01:22.174 --> 00:01:24.813
this is two-methylpropanoic acid.

00:01:26.034 --> 00:01:27.673
Now the boiling points.

00:01:27.954 --> 00:01:30.753
An alcohol's O H can give and take

00:01:30.834 --> 00:01:33.633
hydrogen bonds, and so can the acid's, so

00:01:33.714 --> 00:01:35.553
that alone isn't the difference.

00:01:35.874 --> 00:01:38.273
The difference is the C double bond O

00:01:38.514 --> 00:01:40.433
right beside the O H.

00:01:40.754 --> 00:01:43.313
The O H gives a hydrogen bond, and

00:01:43.354 --> 00:01:45.153
the oxygen of the C double bond O

00:01:45.314 --> 00:01:46.273
takes one.

00:01:46.594 --> 00:01:48.993
So two acid molecules can face each other

00:01:49.154 --> 00:01:51.713
and lock together with two hydrogen bonds.

00:01:52.194 --> 00:01:54.193
That pair is called a dimer.

00:01:54.914 --> 00:01:57.553
Add dipole-dipole forces from the C double bond

00:01:57.714 --> 00:02:00.433
O, and acid molecules attract each other more

00:02:00.514 --> 00:02:01.393
strongly.

00:02:01.714 --> 00:02:04.113
More energy is needed to pull them apart,

00:02:04.194 --> 00:02:05.953
so the acid boils higher.

00:02:07.167 --> 00:02:09.406
Within the family, the pattern is like the

00:02:09.487 --> 00:02:10.446
alcohols.

00:02:10.687 --> 00:02:14.126
Each extra C H two adds dispersion forces,

00:02:14.247 --> 00:02:15.726
so boiling points climb.

00:02:16.367 --> 00:02:19.646
Methanoic acid boils at about one hundred degrees,

00:02:19.727 --> 00:02:22.926
ethanoic at one hundred and eighteen, propanoic at

00:02:23.007 --> 00:02:25.726
one hundred and forty one, and butanoic at

00:02:25.847 --> 00:02:27.966
about one hundred and sixty four.

00:02:28.007 --> 00:02:31.006
The carboxyl group hydrogen bonds with water, so

00:02:31.087 --> 00:02:33.886
the small acids, up to butanoic, mix with

00:02:33.967 --> 00:02:34.926
water.

00:02:35.087 --> 00:02:38.206
After that, the long non-polar chain wins, and

00:02:38.287 --> 00:02:39.806
solubility falls.

00:02:41.034 --> 00:02:42.793
So why is it an acid?

00:02:43.354 --> 00:02:46.073
In water, the O H hydrogen can leave

00:02:46.114 --> 00:02:48.713
as a proton, H plus, and join a

00:02:48.834 --> 00:02:49.793
water molecule.

00:02:50.074 --> 00:02:53.753
That makes hydronium, and an ethanoate ion, C

00:02:53.834 --> 00:02:56.393
H three C O O minus.

00:02:56.434 --> 00:02:59.273
But the reaction is reversible, and the equilibrium

00:02:59.354 --> 00:03:01.113
lies well to the left.

00:03:01.194 --> 00:03:04.633
Ethanoate ions keep taking protons back, so most

00:03:04.674 --> 00:03:06.473
molecules stay whole.

00:03:06.554 --> 00:03:08.873
From the strength video, at point one moles

00:03:08.954 --> 00:03:11.913
per litre, ethanoic acid's pH is about two

00:03:12.074 --> 00:03:13.273
point nine.

00:03:13.354 --> 00:03:15.753
Only about one molecule in eighty has given

00:03:15.834 --> 00:03:17.193
its proton away.

00:03:17.314 --> 00:03:20.153
So ethanoic acid is a weak acid.

00:03:20.234 --> 00:03:23.433
Weak means partly ionised, not dilute.

00:03:24.634 --> 00:03:26.713
But why does the acid's O H let

00:03:26.794 --> 00:03:29.433
go, when an alcohol's barely does?

00:03:29.754 --> 00:03:32.473
Look at what's left behind, the conjugate base.

00:03:33.114 --> 00:03:36.073
When ethanoic acid loses its proton, the C

00:03:36.194 --> 00:03:39.113
double bond O right beside that oxygen lets

00:03:39.194 --> 00:03:42.233
the negative charge spread across both oxygens, about

00:03:42.314 --> 00:03:43.113
half on each.

00:03:43.754 --> 00:03:45.673
Half a charge pulls on a proton less

00:03:45.754 --> 00:03:49.513
strongly, so ethanoate takes protons back less readily.

00:03:50.154 --> 00:03:52.393
An alcohol has no C double bond O

00:03:52.474 --> 00:03:54.713
there, so the whole charge would sit on

00:03:54.794 --> 00:03:57.113
one oxygen, and it would snatch the proton

00:03:57.194 --> 00:03:58.233
straight back.

00:03:58.554 --> 00:04:00.793
That's why ethanoic acid's p K a is

00:04:00.874 --> 00:04:03.593
about four point seven, and an alcohol's is

00:04:03.714 --> 00:04:04.713
about sixteen.

00:04:05.934 --> 00:04:07.453
You can see the difference.

00:04:07.694 --> 00:04:10.413
A drop of ethanoic acid solution turns blue

00:04:10.494 --> 00:04:13.213
litmus red, because the acid has raised the

00:04:13.334 --> 00:04:15.613
hydronium ion concentration.

00:04:15.654 --> 00:04:18.733
A drop of propan-one-ol leaves it blue.

00:04:18.814 --> 00:04:20.973
On its own, the alcohol is about as

00:04:21.054 --> 00:04:22.093
neutral as water.

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:25.620
It also reacts like the acids from Module

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:26.420
six.

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:30.420
With sodium hydroxide, it's neutralised, forming water and

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:32.860
a salt, sodium ethanoate.

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:36.100
The salt's name swaps oic acid for oate.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:39.060
With magnesium, it reacts more slowly than a

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:42.420
strong acid at the same concentration, forming magnesium

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:45.460
ethanoate, and hydrogen gas bubbles off.

00:04:46.667 --> 00:04:50.026
The most useful test is sodium hydrogen carbonate,

00:04:50.187 --> 00:04:51.226
baking soda.

00:04:51.547 --> 00:04:54.346
Ethanoic acid is a stronger acid than carbonic

00:04:54.427 --> 00:04:57.066
acid, so it passes a proton to hydrogen

00:04:57.147 --> 00:04:58.026
carbonate.

00:04:58.347 --> 00:05:00.826
The carbonic acid that forms breaks up into

00:05:00.907 --> 00:05:03.626
water and carbon dioxide, so it fizzes.

00:05:04.267 --> 00:05:06.666
An alcohol is far too weak, so nothing

00:05:06.707 --> 00:05:07.226
happens.

00:05:07.867 --> 00:05:10.506
In this course, if an organic compound fizzes

00:05:10.587 --> 00:05:14.026
with sodium hydrogen carbonate, it's a carboxylic acid.

00:05:14.587 --> 00:05:16.186
Just don't forget the C O two in

00:05:16.227 --> 00:05:17.066
the equation.

00:05:18.267 --> 00:05:20.746
So, a carboxyl group is a C double

00:05:20.827 --> 00:05:23.066
bond O and an O H on one

00:05:23.147 --> 00:05:26.426
carbon, and the names end in oic acid.

00:05:26.747 --> 00:05:30.106
For molecules of similar size, carboxylic acid beats

00:05:30.187 --> 00:05:34.586
alcohol, beats aldehyde or ketone, beats alkane, because

00:05:34.667 --> 00:05:37.186
acid pairs hydrogen bond twice.

00:05:37.467 --> 00:05:39.946
And it's acidic because the charge left behind

00:05:40.027 --> 00:05:42.373
is shared, which loosens its grip on the

00:05:42.427 --> 00:05:43.226
proton.

00:05:43.547 --> 00:05:45.706
Share the charge, loosen the grip.

00:05:46.934 --> 00:05:47.493
Your turn.

00:05:47.574 --> 00:05:51.333
Compound X has the condensed formula shown, and

00:05:51.414 --> 00:05:53.333
it boils at one hundred and seventy seven

00:05:53.414 --> 00:05:54.613
degrees.

00:05:54.774 --> 00:05:57.733
Hexan-one-ol, with a similar molar mass, boils at

00:05:57.814 --> 00:05:59.733
one hundred and fifty seven.

00:05:59.814 --> 00:06:02.293
Give the IUPAC name of X, and explain

00:06:02.374 --> 00:06:04.933
why X has the higher boiling point.

00:06:05.094 --> 00:06:06.293
Pause here to answer.

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:19.420
The carboxyl carbon is carbon one, and the

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:21.900
chain has four carbons, with a methyl on

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:23.340
carbon three.

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:26.740
So X is three-methylbutanoic acid.

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:31.020
Two-methylpropanoic acid leaves out the carboxyl carbon.

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:33.820
For the boiling point, similar molar masses mean

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:37.500
similar dispersion forces, and both hydrogen bond.

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:40.540
But X's molecules form dimers, held by two

00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:44.060
hydrogen bonds, with dipole-dipole forces too.

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:46.460
So more energy is needed to separate them.

00:06:47.667 --> 00:06:48.306
Last one.

00:06:48.947 --> 00:06:53.346
Butanoic acid and butan-one-ol are both colourless liquids.

00:06:53.667 --> 00:06:56.466
Describe a chemical test that would distinguish them,

00:06:56.547 --> 00:06:59.506
including a balanced equation for any reaction.

00:06:59.827 --> 00:07:02.786
Then explain why only one of them reacts.

00:07:03.347 --> 00:07:04.706
Pause here to answer.

00:07:15.134 --> 00:07:17.053
Add sodium hydrogen carbonate.

00:07:17.294 --> 00:07:21.693
Butanoic acid fizzes, giving sodium butanoate, water and

00:07:21.854 --> 00:07:23.093
carbon dioxide.

00:07:23.374 --> 00:07:25.613
Butan-one-ol shows no change.

00:07:26.014 --> 00:07:26.413
Why?

00:07:26.894 --> 00:07:30.093
Butanoate's charge is shared over two oxygens, so

00:07:30.214 --> 00:07:33.053
butanoic acid is a stronger acid than carbonic

00:07:33.134 --> 00:07:36.373
acid, and it gives hydrogen carbonate a proton.

00:07:36.654 --> 00:07:39.293
The alcohol's charge would sit on one oxygen,

00:07:39.374 --> 00:07:40.853
so it's far too weak.

00:07:41.134 --> 00:07:43.373
Saying both react because both have an O

00:07:43.454 --> 00:07:44.813
H is the trap.

00:07:46.034 --> 00:07:48.593
So the carboxyl group explains the high boiling

00:07:48.674 --> 00:07:50.913
points, and why these acids give up a

00:07:50.994 --> 00:07:51.553
proton.

00:07:52.114 --> 00:07:55.313
Next: what happens when a carboxylic acid meets

00:07:55.394 --> 00:07:57.873
an alcohol, and makes something that smells like

00:07:57.954 --> 00:07:58.513
fruit.

00:07:59.734 --> 00:08:01.173
Here are the key notes.

00:08:01.414 --> 00:08:03.333
Pause the video if you need more time

00:08:03.414 --> 00:08:04.213
to copy them.
